# HTML Tutorial Section Rule

When the user says **"apply to \<Section\>"** (or similar, e.g. `@html_tutorial.mdc apply to HTML Introduction`), document that tutorial section from the **open browser tab** (or the site they name, default [W3Schools HTML](https://www.w3schools.com/html/default.asp)).

They may also name a **range**: **"apply to \<Start\> to \<End\>"** (e.g. `apply to HTML Editors to HTML Headings`). Process every left/sidebar item from Start through End **inclusive**, in nav order. One named section = one accordion. A range = one accordion per section in that span. Do **not** crawl the rest of the course, skip items in the range, or continue past End. If Start or End does not match a nav item, list nearby items and stop — do not guess. Finish each section (read → sandbox → run → snap → append) before starting the next.

This output is a **detailed study tutorial**, not a thin recap. The reader must be able to learn the whole section from the accordion alone — without ever opening W3Schools. That means **every idea explained in depth, every site example rebuilt and run, extra clarifying examples added where they help, and every result shown as a snap with a written description of what happened.** When in doubt, add more explanation and more examples, not less.

Match the grain and layout of `Personal/Files/javascript/tutorial.md` (Introduction example list + `[View]` jump links, one `### Example` per Tryit / named tag / table row, code and result snaps beside each example). You can view the HTML output in `Personal/Files/html/tutorial.md`.

## Workflow Steps

1. **Read** `Personal/bin/format.md` once for **accordion spacing and Q&A nesting only**. Ignore its Lab and Code children — they are removed in this rule. This rule’s heading order and Detailed Explanation layout win.
2. **Open the section in the browser**
   - Default site: [https://www.w3schools.com/html/default.asp](https://www.w3schools.com/html/default.asp).
   - Use the existing Cursor browser tab if it is already on that tutorial.
   - If not, navigate to the site, then click the named item in the left/sidebar (or in-page heading). Example: **HTML Introduction**.
   - If the name does not match, list nearby nav items and stop — do not guess a different section.
   - Scroll the **full** section. Read every heading, note, example, exercise, table, and warning. Capture the **section URL**.
3. **Rebuild examples in the sandbox**
   - Create `Personal/Files/html/code_sandbox/<slug>/` (`HTML Introduction` → `html-introduction`).
   - Write runnable files. Prefer `index.html`. Extra `.css` / `.js` only if the section uses them. Extra HTML files when the page has several Tryit examples.
   - Recreate **every** Tryit / runnable example **and every reference-table row** on the page. One HTML file per Example. Do not invent unrelated features, and do not drop examples or table rows to save space. You **may** add small extra variations when they make a concept clearer — label them as your own example.
   - If the section has **no** runnable example, skip the run/snap steps and say so in Detailed Explanation.
4. **Run the example**
   - Serve the sandbox over **http** (Cursor browser blocks `file://`). From `code_sandbox`, use a local static server, then open `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<slug>/`.
   - Confirm the page matches the tutorial example (heading, paragraph, output).
   - If the example **errors** or will not run, research a fix — prefer **official docs** (MDN HTML, WHATWG HTML Living Standard). Tutorial sites often keep older snippets after the spec or browsers have moved on. Use the current correct markup in the sandbox, and note the mismatch in Detailed Explanation (what the page showed vs what works now). Do not invent unrelated features.
   - Stop the server when snaps are done.
5. **Snap source and result**
   - **Result:** screenshot the **running** sandbox page in the Cursor browser. That is the outcome the reader must see.
   - **Code:** snap the sandbox source. Prefer **CodeSnap** (`CodeSnap 📸` / `codesnap.start`) on the editor selection. If the agent cannot fire CodeSnap, screenshot the example as shown (tutorial editor or sandbox source) and still save a PNG.
   - Save PNGs under `Personal/Files/html/code_sandbox/snaps/`. Use **per-example numbering** so each example has its own pair:
     - `<slug>-01-code.png`, `<slug>-02-code.png`, …
     - `<slug>-01-result.png`, `<slug>-02-result.png`, …
     - A single-example section may use `<slug>-code.png` / `<slug>-result.png`.
   - Embed **relative** images from `tutorial.md`: `./code_sandbox/snaps/<file>.png`
6. **Append** one formatted accordion per section to `Personal/Files/html/tutorial.md`. **Never overwrite** the file. If an accordion with the same `<summary>` already exists, ask before replacing that block.
7. If `tutorial.md` has no `#` title yet, start the file with `# HTML Tutorial` then the accordion.

## Format Requirements

Accordion **spacing** matches `Personal/.cursor/rules/notes_doc.mdc` and `Personal/bin/format.md`. Heading **order and children** follow this rule:

- Topic accordion `<summary>` = the section title the user named (e.g. `HTML Introduction`).
- **Accordion spacing:** one blank line after every `</summary>` and one blank line before every `</details>`.
- **Introduction**, **Detailed Explanation**, **Summary**, **References** = plain `##` headings (not child accordions).
- **Introduction must list every Example title.** After the short prose (what the section is), list **all** `### **Example N: Title**` headings in order — if the section has 34 examples, list all 34. Do not summarize, skip, or group them. Each line ends with a **`[View](#…)`** link that scrolls to that example. Use this shape (no code in the Introduction):

```markdown
This section has **N** examples:

- [x] **Example 1:** Title of example 1 [View](#html-introduction-example-01)
- [x] **Example 2:** Title of example 2 [View](#html-introduction-example-02)
- [x] **Example N:** Title of example N [View](#html-introduction-example-NN)
```

  The titles must match the Detailed Explanation headings exactly. The **View** target is `#<slug>-example-NN` (slug from the section title: `HTML Introduction` → `html-introduction`; NN is two digits). Place `<a id="<slug>-example-NN"></a>` immediately before the matching `### **Example N:**` heading so the link scrolls there. This list is a table of contents so the reader can see the whole section at a glance.
- After Detailed Explanation, only these **closed child accordions**, in this order:
  1. **Terminal Commands**
  2. **Questions and Answers**
- **No `Lab` section.** **No separate `Code` section.** All prose, fenced markup, code snaps, and result snaps live **inside Detailed Explanation**, next to the idea they demonstrate.
- Q&A: **8–15** questions that cover the actual examples (tags, attributes, rendered output, pitfalls), not just definitions; each answer is nested `<details><summary>Answer</summary>` with **only** `- [x]` lines.
- First References link = the **section URL** from the browser. Then other links from that page (and MDN / WHATWG when you used them).
- If a snap file is missing, do not fake an `<img>` path. Finish the snaps first.

### Detailed Explanation (the merged study body)

This is the heart of the tutorial and absorbs what used to be the separate **Code** section. Write it as a **guided, example-driven walkthrough**. Start with a short `- [x]` concept list, then walk **every** example from the page in order (plus any extra variations you add). **For each example, always include all five parts, in this order:**

1. **Heading** — `<a id="<slug>-example-NN"></a>` then `### **Example N: Title**` (title from the page, or a clear label). The `id` is what Introduction `[View](#<slug>-example-NN)` scrolls to.
2. **Explanation** — `- [x]` lines describing **what the markup does, why those tags/attributes exist, and what the browser shows** (nesting, required attributes, defaults, accessibility notes, "do not" advice). Be generous: multiple bullets and nested sub-points, not one sentence. Explain any table, note, or warning here in the same style.
3. **Tested code** — a fenced block (`html` / `css` / `javascript`) with the **actual sandbox source that ran** (never an untested paste from the site). Mention the sandbox path, e.g. `Sandbox: code_sandbox/<slug>/index.html`.
4. **Code snap** — `<img ... src="./code_sandbox/snaps/<slug>-NN-code.png" />` right after the fence.
5. **Result snap + written outcome** — `<img ... src="./code_sandbox/snaps/<slug>-NN-result.png" />`, immediately followed by at least one `- [x]` line that **describes the rendered outcome in words** ("a large bold heading over a paragraph", "a bulleted list of three items"). The image must never be the only explanation of the result.

Cover **all** examples this way — do not summarize several into one. If official docs required newer markup than the page, keep **both**: the page’s idea and the working current form, and say how they differ. Prefer thoroughness: more examples and richer explanation is the goal.

### Grain: one named thing = one `### Example` (HTML Basic is the model)

**HTML Basic** is the required grain: the page named several constructs, and **each** gets its own heading (`Example 1: A full document`, `Example 2: Headings`, `Example 3: Paragraphs`, `Example 4: Links`, `Example 5: Images`) with its own sandbox file, snaps, and outcome. Copy that pattern everywhere. Same idea as **JS Output** in the JavaScript tutorial.

**Never** list many tags / attributes / elements in a bullet and then show **one** representative snippet. That is how **HTML Formatting** used to dump `<b>`, `<strong>`, `<i>`, `<em>`, … under one intro demo. Listing names is not teaching them.

Walk the page in order and emit a **separate** `### **Example N: Title**` (all five parts) for **each** of:

1. **Every Tryit / runnable example** on the page — including several Tryits of the *same* tag with different attributes (`<img width>`, `<img alt>` missing, `<a target="_blank">` are **separate** Examples).
2. **Every row of a reference table** (HTML Element / Attribute / Input Type / Event reference). One row = one Example. Use the table’s Name as the heading (`Example 1: <a>`, `Example 2: href`, …).
3. **Every named construct** the page is teaching (`<!DOCTYPE>`, `<h1>`–`<h6>`, `<p>`, `<a>`, `<img>`, each formatting tag, each form control, …) even if the page only shows a tag list — still build a small runnable demo.
4. **Every heading/section** that introduces a distinct idea (notes, warnings, “do not use”, exercises). Deprecated elements (`<font>`, `<center>`, plug-ins) still get an Example that **runs** them and says not to use them; do not skip the row.

**Reference / tag-table pages (highest risk).** If the page is a catalog, you are not done until **every table row** has its own Example. You may **not**:

- Collapse the table into “useful names” / “see the site for the full list”
- Ship one sandbox that prints many results under a single Example heading
- Pick a “typical” tag (`<p>`, `<b>`, `<input type="text">`) as a stand-in for the rest
- Group unrelated tags into one fence unless they are a **single** Tryit on the page

**Sandbox layout:** one HTML file per Example (like `html-basic/headings.html`, `html-formatting/b.html`), plus `index.html` linking them. Number snaps `<slug>-NN-code.png` / `<slug>-NN-result.png` in the **same order** as the Example headings. A single combined dump page is not a substitute.

**Q&A** must ask about **several different Examples** (tags, attributes, rendered output), not five generic definitions while 30 tags went undemoed. Still 8–15 questions; spread them across the page.

